#ifndef ESBMC_REGEX
#define ESBMC_REGEX

// C++11 header (#7797). libc++ ships it in every language mode and expands it
// to nothing in an older one, so an unconditional include still compiles
// (#3387).
//
// The matching itself is not modelled: a regular-expression engine unrolled
// into SSA is not something a solver can carry, and a *wrong* engine would
// prove properties that do not hold. Every function that reports a match
// returns a nondeterministic answer, every sub_match past the whole match is
// nondeterministically participating, and its iterators and text are
// unconstrained. A property that depends on a particular pattern matching is
// therefore reported FAILED rather than proved: incomplete, not unsound.
//
// The one bound that remains is <string>'s: a modelled basic_string holds at
// most STRING_CAPACITY characters, so `result.size() < STRING_CAPACITY` is
// provable of matched text -- as it is of every string in this tree, not just
// of these. Nothing here is tighter than that.
//
// Exact, by contrast: the flag bitmasks, regex_error's code, mark_count()
// including bracket expressions, rejection of an unbalanced pattern, and the
// agreement between a match_results and the call that filled it.
//
// Not modelled, each failing at compile time: regex_iterator and
// regex_token_iterator, which would have to walk real match positions;
// sub_match's relational operators; match_results::position and its
// comparisons; regex_replace's OutputIt form; regex_traits' collation and
// character-class interface beyond what basic_regex needs; and the
// allocator-extended match_results constructors.
#if __cplusplus >= 201103L

